WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.760
I want you to take a breath right now and just look around wherever you happen to be.

00:04.060 --> 00:04.680
Just take a second.

00:04.840 --> 00:05.380
Yeah, exactly.

00:05.860 --> 00:12.700
Take a second and think about the exact physical mechanism of how my voice is getting into your head at this very moment.

00:12.880 --> 00:16.280
Are you wearing earbuds on a really crowded train?

00:16.580 --> 00:16.820
Right.

00:17.260 --> 00:21.600
Or is this playing through your car stereo while you're navigating horrible traffic?

00:21.920 --> 00:28.640
Or maybe you're just sitting alone in a quiet room, you know, reading a written transcript of this conversation on a glowing little rectangle.

00:28.640 --> 00:35.640
Because before we say a single word about our actual topic for this deep dive, I really want you to sit with a deeply uncomfortable proposition.

00:36.460 --> 00:37.480
It is deeply unmarkable.

00:37.640 --> 00:38.260
It really is.

00:39.080 --> 00:46.700
What if the format you are using right now, the app, the audio, the screen, is secretly deciding what you believe is true?

00:47.280 --> 00:56.980
Like what if the medium you chose to consume this information isn't just delivering a message to you, but is actively rewiring your threshold for reality?

00:56.980 --> 01:08.520
I mean, I would argue it is arguably the most urgent question of our entire current media landscape because we spend so much time arguing about the content of our information, right?

01:08.600 --> 01:10.020
Yeah, the politics, the biases.

01:10.260 --> 01:10.700
Exactly.

01:10.860 --> 01:12.820
The fake news versus the real news.

01:13.040 --> 01:18.100
But we are almost completely blind to the actual filtration system itself.

01:18.300 --> 01:20.320
We don't even see the water we're swimming in.

01:20.320 --> 01:21.560
We really don't.

01:21.640 --> 01:30.860
In a world defined by just this overwhelming infinite deluge of data, the biggest risk to our minds isn't actually what we consume.

01:31.280 --> 01:31.680
Okay.

01:31.800 --> 01:32.500
So what is it then?

01:32.680 --> 01:41.900
The existential risk is what has to happen to that information, like how it has to be dressed up and repackaged before you and I are even willing to accept it as meaningful or even real.

01:41.900 --> 01:46.160
And that perfectly brings us to the actual subject of today's Deep Dive.

01:46.220 --> 01:46.560
Yes.

01:47.180 --> 01:52.180
We are looking at this groundbreaking 2026 paper by the independent researcher, Fliction.

01:52.520 --> 01:54.520
Which is just a massive piece of work.

01:54.620 --> 01:55.500
It is staggering.

01:55.740 --> 02:00.940
It's titled, Modality Heading, Communication, Cognitive Operators, and Dependence on Form.

02:01.020 --> 02:02.460
A very academic title.

02:02.520 --> 02:03.620
A very dense title.

02:03.840 --> 02:06.360
And I have to tell you, reading this thing was just an experience.

02:06.540 --> 02:06.760
I bet.

02:06.760 --> 02:19.300
It completely shattered how I view my own media diet, because it starts with this really dense information theory, but then it pulls in, like the psychological trauma from that old TV show, Mr. Robot.

02:19.500 --> 02:19.840
Right.

02:19.960 --> 02:20.760
The hacker show.

02:20.860 --> 02:21.000
Yeah.

02:21.080 --> 02:27.000
And then somehow, out of nowhere, it connects that to the slapstick 90s cartoon Darkwing Duck.

02:27.380 --> 02:28.360
Which is wild.

02:28.640 --> 02:29.160
It is wild.

02:29.320 --> 02:33.700
And then it eventually builds to this massive critique of modern AI chatbots.

02:34.020 --> 02:35.240
It's so ambitious.

02:35.240 --> 02:36.640
It connects so many dots.

02:37.060 --> 02:37.620
It really does.

02:38.180 --> 02:48.980
But to even begin to understand how our favorite formats are kind of trapping us, we have to start by tearing down a very old, very stubborn assumption about how humans communicate.

02:49.220 --> 02:49.460
Yeah.

02:49.640 --> 02:53.980
We really have to dismantle what Fliction calls the myth of the neutral channel.

02:54.120 --> 02:54.840
The neutral channel.

02:54.940 --> 02:55.100
Right.

02:55.120 --> 02:55.340
Okay.

02:55.560 --> 03:02.120
Because to understand how a format can become a psychological prep, we first have to unlearn this idea of the neutral pipe.

03:02.240 --> 03:02.700
The pike.

03:02.860 --> 03:03.120
Yes.

03:03.120 --> 03:08.740
This goes all the way back to 1948 to a really brilliant mathematician named Claude Shannon.

03:08.800 --> 03:09.480
Right, Claude Shannon.

03:09.540 --> 03:12.920
He published basically the foundational work on information theory.

03:13.260 --> 03:17.100
And just to ground this for you listening, Shannon wasn't a psychologist, right?

03:17.300 --> 03:18.020
No, not at all.

03:18.020 --> 03:22.760
He was dealing with hard engineering problems, telegraphs, telephone lines.

03:22.960 --> 03:26.180
Literally just getting a radio signal from point A to point B.

03:26.280 --> 03:26.640
Exactly.

03:26.640 --> 03:37.960
So Shannon was trying to optimize telecommunications, and in his model, communication is essentially just a message that is inserted into a channel, literally like pouring water into a pipe.

03:38.200 --> 03:38.460
Okay.

03:38.700 --> 03:42.840
The channel might be a copper wire or radio frequency or whatever.

03:43.160 --> 03:43.300
Yeah.

03:43.300 --> 03:46.460
Now, that pipe might introduce some noise, right?

03:46.460 --> 03:47.840
No, it's static on the line.

03:48.220 --> 03:48.700
Exactly.

03:48.840 --> 03:50.220
You might get a degraded signal.

03:51.000 --> 03:57.920
But crucially, in Shannon's model, the message itself remains conceptually completely untouched by the pipe.

03:58.120 --> 04:00.400
Meaning the water doesn't change just because it's in a pipe.

04:00.560 --> 04:00.940
Exactly.

04:01.120 --> 04:05.020
The message exists completely independently prior to entering the channel.

04:05.480 --> 04:10.340
And the only goal of the channel is to just spit it out on the other side with as much fidelity as possible.

04:10.340 --> 04:14.200
I am trying to picture this. It's basically how we think about the postal service, isn't it?

04:14.340 --> 04:16.200
That is a really good way to look at it.

04:16.420 --> 04:22.120
Like, if I write a letter to my friend, the meaning of that letter exists the second I put the cap back on my pen.

04:22.180 --> 04:23.480
Right. The meaning is finished.

04:23.600 --> 04:26.440
Yeah. I fold it up. I put it in an envelope. I hand it to the post office.

04:26.560 --> 04:26.680
Right.

04:26.900 --> 04:28.420
The mail truck is the channel.

04:28.500 --> 04:28.660
Yeah.

04:28.940 --> 04:36.440
As long as the postal worker doesn't, you know, drop it in a puddle or tear the envelope, the meaning of the letter is exactly the same when my friend opens it.

04:36.600 --> 04:38.380
The mail truck didn't write the letter.

04:38.380 --> 04:43.360
That is the perfect analogy for how we intuitively view media.

04:43.640 --> 04:46.600
We really do think of formats as mail trucks.

04:46.760 --> 04:46.880
Right.

04:47.160 --> 05:01.380
But Flixion argues that while Shannon's model is this absolute triumph for engineering, I mean, it is literally the reason our cell phones and the internet even work it, is a catastrophic failure when we apply it to human understanding.

05:02.160 --> 05:03.840
Really? A catastrophic failure?

05:03.920 --> 05:06.720
Yes, because human cognition is not a copper wire.

05:07.000 --> 05:07.580
Oh, okay.

05:07.580 --> 05:19.080
In human communication, a sentence can be transmitted perfectly, you know, with zero noise, perfect audio, or perfect visual fidelity, and yet it can still be entirely misunderstood.

05:19.320 --> 05:20.560
Even if the words are perfect.

05:20.980 --> 05:28.580
Exactly. Or it can be aestheticized or dismissed as totally trivial, entirely based on the form in which it arrives.

05:28.580 --> 05:38.560
Wait, hold on. Let me unpack this. If the fidelity is perfect, if the words are exactly the same, how can the form change the meaning? The words are just the words.

05:38.560 --> 05:42.020
Because for human beings, the form isn't just an envelope.

05:42.440 --> 05:42.660
Okay.

05:42.740 --> 05:47.620
It doesn't just transport meaning. It actually creates the conditions for the meaning to even exist.

05:47.760 --> 05:48.640
That is a big claim.

05:48.820 --> 05:49.280
It is.

05:49.740 --> 05:53.540
And Flixion introduces a concept here called a modality.

05:53.760 --> 05:54.600
A modality.

05:54.800 --> 06:04.940
Right. A modality is any form of representation. So it could be prose, mathematical notation, a comic book, a piece of music, a conversational interface, a spreadsheet.

06:04.940 --> 06:07.120
Basically any way information is packaged.

06:07.320 --> 06:12.200
Yes. And crucially, a modality is not a carrier. It is an operator.

06:12.400 --> 06:20.960
An operator. I need you to break that down. Because when I hear operator, I literally think of someone plugging cables into like a 1920s telephone switchboard.

06:21.080 --> 06:26.540
Yeah, that's not quite it. Think of it more like a mathematical operator, like a plus sign or a square root symbol.

06:26.680 --> 06:27.300
Okay, math.

06:27.540 --> 06:31.800
Right. An operator does something active to the material it touches. It transforms it.

06:31.800 --> 06:37.540
The paper actually gives a very helpful, albeit pretty abstract, schematic for this.

06:37.720 --> 06:39.900
Yeah. The equation. I have this circled in my note.

06:39.940 --> 06:44.460
It is an equation. R equals M, parenthesis C, comma A, comma E.

06:44.480 --> 06:54.040
Okay. R equals MCAE. I am staring at this formula right now. Walk me through exactly what this means in practical terms. Because looking at letters doesn't help me.

06:54.120 --> 06:58.120
I get it. Let's define the variables. The R is the received object.

06:58.360 --> 06:59.320
The received object.

06:59.320 --> 07:04.220
Yes. This is what actually forms in your mind the final understanding you actually walk away with.

07:04.300 --> 07:04.480
Okay.

07:04.940 --> 07:09.160
The M is the modality, the format. So let's say it's a short form video on social media.

07:09.360 --> 07:09.740
Got it.

07:09.880 --> 07:17.200
Now that modality is actively operating on three things inside those parentheses. First is C, the communicative material.

07:17.460 --> 07:18.200
The raw facts.

07:18.200 --> 07:24.360
Exactly. The raw information, the raw events of reality. Second is A, the agent.

07:24.520 --> 07:25.020
Which is me.

07:25.240 --> 07:26.500
That's you, the human receiver.

07:26.680 --> 07:26.780
Yeah.

07:26.800 --> 07:31.620
With all of your preexisting biases, your attention span, your current emotional state.

07:31.760 --> 07:38.960
And finally, E is the environment, the physical and cultural context in which you are receiving the message.

07:38.960 --> 07:42.460
Okay. I want to play with this formula for a second to see if I really get it.

07:42.560 --> 07:42.960
Go for it.

07:43.060 --> 07:48.980
Let's say the C, the material, is a breaking news event, like a terrible earthquake somewhere.

07:49.180 --> 07:50.040
Okay. An earthquake.

07:50.360 --> 08:03.400
If the modality M is a 50-page geological report, the R, my received object, is going to be incredibly detailed, structural knowledge, but honestly zero emotional connection.

08:03.580 --> 08:04.420
Very analytical.

08:04.740 --> 08:08.500
Right. But if you keep the C exactly the same, it's still the exact same earthquake.

08:08.740 --> 08:08.900
Yeah.

08:09.060 --> 08:13.660
But you change the M to a 10-second video of a weeping survivor on my phone.

08:13.900 --> 08:15.420
The R changes completely.

08:15.560 --> 08:22.440
Yes. The R, the thing I walk away with, is purely emotional urgency. The material didn't change at all. The operator changed.

08:22.640 --> 08:29.620
Precisely. And let's adjust another variable just to show how powerful this is. Let's keep the material C the same and the modality M the same.

08:29.900 --> 08:32.300
So it's still the 10-second emotional video of the earthquake.

08:32.300 --> 08:34.840
Exactly. But let's change the agent, the A.

08:34.960 --> 08:43.460
Let's say you are an exhausted, incredibly cynical trauma surgeon who sees tragedy literally every single day.

08:43.640 --> 08:44.180
Wow. Okay.

08:44.400 --> 08:53.260
Your R, your received understanding of that video, will be entirely different from the R of a highly sheltered teenager seeing it for the first time.

08:53.340 --> 08:54.100
Oh, that makes sense.

08:54.340 --> 09:00.460
The modality operates on the material, but it also operates on the specific cognitive constraints of the agent.

09:00.460 --> 09:03.900
And the E, the environment. How does that actually change the equation?

09:04.360 --> 09:12.220
Well, imagine consuming a really dense political manifesto. The material C is the text. The modality M is a physical book.

09:12.340 --> 09:12.560
Okay.

09:12.560 --> 09:24.420
If your environment E is a quiet university library and you are surrounded by scholars, the modality operates very differently than if your environment is a chaotic, incredibly loud subway train.

09:24.500 --> 09:26.660
I'm not going to process a manifesto on the subway.

09:26.840 --> 09:31.240
Right. The environment dictates how much cognitive friction you can actually afford to push through.

09:31.240 --> 09:36.560
So the modality interacts with all of these factors to produce the final apprehended reality.

09:36.720 --> 09:41.720
Okay. I think I follow the equation, but I want to push back on this underlying idea of the operator.

09:42.140 --> 09:49.620
Because honestly, isn't this just a very fancy, hypofacademic way of restating the old cliche that presentation matters?

09:49.980 --> 09:51.120
People say that a lot. Yeah.

09:51.120 --> 10:02.280
Like if I read a dry black and white court transcript of two people arguing versus hearing the actual audio recording of them screaming and crying, obviously the audio adds emotion.

10:02.720 --> 10:03.740
It gives you more context.

10:03.880 --> 10:11.860
It gives me more data. But how does that make the audio an operator that constitutes meaning? It just seems like a much more detailed mail truck.

10:12.020 --> 10:17.460
That is a very common objection. And fliction draws a really vital distinction here between two concepts.

10:17.460 --> 10:18.320
Okay. What are they?

10:18.460 --> 10:22.540
The difference between ease of reception versus adequacy of representation.

10:22.900 --> 10:23.700
Ease of reception.

10:23.880 --> 10:32.860
Right. When we colloquially say that presentation matters, we usually just mean that a good presentation makes an independent fact easier to digest.

10:33.240 --> 10:34.580
Like putting sugar on medicine.

10:34.840 --> 10:41.540
Exactly. We assume the fact is just sitting there, whole and perfect, and a nice colorful graph just makes it easier to swallow.

10:41.540 --> 10:49.540
But an operator does not just decorate or deliver meaning. It decides what relationships are even theoretically possible to see.

10:49.700 --> 10:55.500
Give me a concrete example of a format dictating what I'm allowed to see. Because that sounds crazy.

10:55.760 --> 10:57.280
Let's look at mathematical notation.

10:57.560 --> 10:57.980
Oh boy.

10:58.400 --> 11:07.520
Stick with me. Imagine I want to describe the really complex intersecting gravitational pulls of an entire solar system.

11:07.520 --> 11:08.040
Okay.

11:08.440 --> 11:14.740
If I am forced to use the modality of conversational English to do that, it will take me hundreds of pages.

11:15.020 --> 11:17.060
Oh yeah. You'd have to write out every single orbit.

11:17.120 --> 11:19.320
I will have to describe every orbit in prose.

11:20.160 --> 11:27.400
And because human working memory is limited, by the time you read page 50, you will have totally forgotten the relationships I established on page 2.

11:27.520 --> 11:28.160
That's so true.

11:28.520 --> 11:35.680
The English language is fundamentally inadequate for representing simultaneous multivariable structural relations.

11:35.680 --> 11:41.360
Right. The language itself forces me to process it sequentially. Just word by word, line by line.

11:41.520 --> 11:47.440
Exactly. Now let's change the modality. If I use mathematical notation, I drop all the context of ordinary language.

11:47.480 --> 11:48.660
You lose the verbs and nouns.

11:49.100 --> 11:58.080
I strip away tone, history, narrative flow, all of it. But what I gain is this absolute miracle of cognitive engineering.

11:58.080 --> 12:04.500
I make pure structural simultaneous relations inspectable at a single glance.

12:04.740 --> 12:06.000
Because I can see the whole equation at once.

12:06.060 --> 12:12.580
Yes. I can see the entire system on a whiteboard. The math notation isn't just making the English easier to read.

12:12.700 --> 12:13.560
No, it's totally different.

12:13.760 --> 12:24.340
It is operating on the raw material of reality to produce a totally different kind of cognitive object. It basically destroys narrative to preserve structure.

12:24.340 --> 12:27.840
Oh, wow. Okay. So the format is always a trade-off.

12:27.960 --> 12:28.240
Yes.

12:28.440 --> 12:32.840
It's like a deal with the devil. You get something you really want, but you have to murder something else to get it.

12:32.940 --> 12:38.720
Always. Every single translation from one modality to another preserves certain features and just annihilates others.

12:39.300 --> 12:43.400
Take the medium of comic books, which Fliction explicitly analyzes in the paper.

12:43.640 --> 12:44.320
Comics. Okay.

12:44.540 --> 12:46.500
How does a comic book handle time?

12:46.620 --> 12:47.880
Well, I mean, it freezes it, right?

12:47.880 --> 12:55.300
It does way more than freeze it. It takes time, which is a sequence of events, and it distributes it across physical space on a page.

12:55.580 --> 12:58.080
Oh, right. Because panel one is before panel two.

12:58.400 --> 13:08.800
Think about the cognitive power of that. In a comic, you, the reader, can actually control the duration of inspection without changing the represented order of events.

13:09.060 --> 13:09.440
What do you mean?

13:09.440 --> 13:23.420
You can linger on a single, highly detailed, tragic panel for five minutes, just examining the artwork, analyzing the geometry of the scene, without altering the fact that in the actual story, this moment only took a split second.

13:23.680 --> 13:24.940
And a movie doesn't let you do that.

13:25.160 --> 13:25.720
Not at all.

13:26.000 --> 13:36.980
A movie dictates the time. If the director gives that moment three seconds of screen time, you only get three seconds. If you pause the movie to look at the background, you break the modality. The movie stops being a movie.

13:36.980 --> 13:44.140
Precisely. The comic modality creates a cognitive object where time is spadiotemporally navigable by the user.

13:44.360 --> 13:46.900
Which is a fancy way of saying you can look around in time.

13:47.080 --> 14:01.140
Exactly. But the cinematic modality creates an object where time is rigidly enforced by the creator. The medium determines what kind of object can be apprehended at all, and we often mistake the affordances of the medium for the properties of the truth itself.

14:01.400 --> 14:04.520
What do you mean by that? Mistaking the medium for the truth?

14:04.520 --> 14:17.220
Let's say a beautifully designed infographic takes a wildly complex, really messy geopolitical conflict, and it reduces it to a clean, three-color pie chart.

14:17.480 --> 14:18.880
We see that on the news every day.

14:19.080 --> 14:25.220
Right. We might look at that chart and falsely conclude, ah, this conflict is actually very simple.

14:25.560 --> 14:26.160
Oh, I see.

14:26.160 --> 14:37.760
We mistake the simplicity of the diagram for the simplicity of the world. We completely forget that the diagram only looks simple because the modality of the infographic literally demanded the exclusion of all the messy variables.

14:38.020 --> 14:47.720
That makes perfect sense. And it sets us up brilliantly for how this paper moves from the abstract math of information theory to something incredibly visceral and visible.

14:47.720 --> 14:49.200
It really gets tangible here.

14:49.200 --> 14:59.480
It does. Because before we even get to complex infographics or movies, Flicking points out that this operator effect happens at the literal surface level of language.

14:59.580 --> 14:59.780
Yes.

14:59.800 --> 15:02.880
I'm talking about the actual shapes of the letters you're reading right now, the fonts.

15:03.380 --> 15:03.860
Typography.

15:04.180 --> 15:07.420
The paper introduces this phenomenal term, typeface heading.

15:07.620 --> 15:08.780
Which is such a good term.

15:09.180 --> 15:13.360
It's so good. Which is a play on the term wire heading, right?

15:13.360 --> 15:19.400
Yes. To understand typeface heading, we really have to understand the biological roots of wire heading.

15:19.640 --> 15:20.560
Okay. Take us through it.

15:20.940 --> 15:33.760
Wire heading is a concept from neuroscience. In these classic, somewhat disturbing experiments, researchers would implant an electrode directly into the pleasure center of an animal's brain, say, a rat.

15:33.840 --> 15:34.120
Okay.

15:34.200 --> 15:38.120
They would connect that electrode to a little lever in the cage.

15:38.120 --> 15:43.700
Now, usually an animal has to perform a real-world behavior to get a dopamine reward, right?

15:43.980 --> 15:46.700
It has to hunt for food or mate or solve a puzzle.

15:46.960 --> 15:55.780
Exactly. But with the wire head set up, the animal can just press the lever and get the neurochemical reward directly, bypassing reality entirely.

15:55.980 --> 15:57.000
It just hacks the system.

15:57.200 --> 16:06.000
Yes. It just sits there, pressing the lever until it literally stars to death, because the feeling of success has been totally decoupled from actual survival success.

16:06.220 --> 16:07.380
That is extremely dark.

16:07.380 --> 16:08.000
It is.

16:08.080 --> 16:12.620
But typeface heading is doing that exact same decoupling, but with intellectual meaning instead of food.

16:12.720 --> 16:13.000
Exactly.

16:13.160 --> 16:27.360
The paper defines it as when the visual treatment of language produces the effect, the bodily emotional feeling of semantic significance, before you have done the actual cognitive work to read and assess the claim.

16:27.680 --> 16:29.760
You are feeling smart before you are smart.

16:29.980 --> 16:34.700
Yes. You're judging a book by its font, and your brain is just giving you a dopamine hit for it.

16:34.700 --> 16:44.260
It is the manufacturing of an intellectual aura. The lexical content, like the actual dictionary definition of the words on the page, can remain exactly the same.

16:44.320 --> 16:45.220
Words don't change.

16:45.220 --> 16:54.560
Right. But if you change the typeface, the spacing, the alignment, the margins, you completely alter the presumptive authority of the text.

16:54.780 --> 16:58.000
You trigger a totally different cognitive expectation in the agent.

16:58.000 --> 17:02.520
And Flickton provides several incredibly evocative examples to prove this point.

17:02.560 --> 17:04.140
They do, and they are fascinating.

17:04.340 --> 17:11.780
Let's go through them, because this was the exact moment in the paper where I started feeling a little paranoid about how easily manipulated my own brain is.

17:11.780 --> 17:16.060
We all are. Imagine encountering a perfectly ordinary phrase on the internet, let's say.

17:16.420 --> 17:17.940
The system is functioning normally.

17:18.300 --> 17:19.660
A boring IT phrase.

17:20.040 --> 17:25.400
Right. But it is rendered using dense, stacked Unicode characters.

17:26.360 --> 17:30.360
This is often referred to in internet culture as Zalgo text.

17:30.460 --> 17:32.040
Oh, I've seen that. It looks chaotic.

17:32.320 --> 17:39.460
It does. There are all these weird phonetic diacritics just bleeding vertically up and down into the other lines of text.

17:39.460 --> 17:43.480
Right. It looks glitchy, like a computer virus is eating the words from the inside out.

17:43.580 --> 17:44.020
Yes.

17:44.480 --> 17:47.160
It looks haunted or, like, computationally unstable.

17:47.340 --> 17:49.940
Exactly. But notice what is actually happening.

17:50.540 --> 17:54.420
The lexical meaning of the text just says the system is functioning normally.

17:54.740 --> 17:55.900
But I don't believe it.

17:55.980 --> 17:59.580
Right. Because the graphical form is performing instability.

17:59.960 --> 18:03.580
The visual operator contradicts the linguistic message.

18:03.680 --> 18:04.500
That's so weird.

18:04.640 --> 18:07.640
And that performative coincidence is incredibly powerful.

18:07.640 --> 18:13.420
The reader encounters an inscription that seems to basically exceed its own encoding.

18:14.080 --> 18:20.140
It feels dangerous or transgressive simply because of the math used to render the pixels on the screen.

18:20.400 --> 18:22.940
And it goes the other way, too, right? Towards manufacturing authority.

18:23.060 --> 18:23.620
Absolutely.

18:24.300 --> 18:33.220
Consider monospace text, where every single letter, whether it's a wide W or a really narrow I, takes up the exact same horizontal width.

18:33.400 --> 18:34.180
Like a typewriter.

18:34.180 --> 18:40.480
Yes. When you see monospace text on a screen, what does your brain instantly associate it with?

18:40.600 --> 18:44.740
Computer programming. Like terminals, command lines.

18:44.960 --> 18:45.480
The matrix.

18:45.720 --> 18:48.220
Yeah, exactly. Green text falling down a black screen.

18:48.260 --> 18:52.460
Right. It evokes the raw, unvarnished output of a machine.

18:52.460 --> 18:59.980
So if you want to claim to feel objective, data-driven, and totally devoid of human bias, you write it in a monospace font.

19:00.140 --> 19:01.700
Because it looks like a computer thought it.

19:01.820 --> 19:07.180
Yes. Your brain receives the effect of objectivity way before you've analyzed the actual data.

19:07.280 --> 19:07.920
That's crazy.

19:08.260 --> 19:15.880
Similarly, if you use a heavy black letter or gothic font, it instantly evokes historical weight, liturgy, tradition, or even militant nationalism.

19:16.040 --> 19:17.200
Like an old newspaper header.

19:17.200 --> 19:26.220
Exactly. If you use a clean, geometric sans-serif font like Helvetica, it evokes administrative modernity, hygiene, corporate efficiency, technical precision.

19:26.660 --> 19:30.240
Okay. Here's the part of this argument that really, really clicked for me.

19:31.220 --> 19:37.780
The paper makes this incredibly sharp distinction between inscriptional evidence and inscriptional simulation.

19:37.980 --> 19:39.020
Yes. This is key.

19:39.020 --> 19:52.240
Because sometimes a text really has been through the ringer, right? If I go to a museum and look at a historical manuscript and there are crossed out words, ink smudges, notes scribbled in the margins by monks.

19:52.500 --> 19:54.340
Right. Physical wear and tear.

19:54.400 --> 20:00.940
Yeah. That is inscriptional evidence of the revision process. The document actually survived a very difficult history.

20:01.060 --> 20:01.920
It earned those marks.

20:01.980 --> 20:02.440
It earned them.

20:02.560 --> 20:02.700
Yeah.

20:02.880 --> 20:08.720
But what typeface heading relies on is inscriptional simulation. It's faking the residue of difficulty.

20:08.720 --> 20:14.720
That is a crucial and really profound distinction. Inscriptional simulation introduces these features deliberately.

20:15.520 --> 20:16.120
It's a trickus.

20:16.320 --> 20:26.720
An author might use really obscure specialist mathematical symbols when simple words would do just fine, or deliberately damage characters in a digital art piece, or use elaborate cryptic marginalia.

20:27.520 --> 20:28.340
Just to look cool.

20:28.680 --> 20:37.940
They do this to evoke a history of transmission, secrecy, or elite technical refinement without the document actually having survived that history at all.

20:37.940 --> 20:41.940
It is a purely aesthetic shortcut to authority.

20:42.340 --> 20:45.540
Wait. I'm trying to map this onto something familiar to the physical world.

20:45.600 --> 20:45.720
Okay.

20:45.720 --> 20:49.360
Is this kind of like buying pre-distressed jeans?

20:49.620 --> 20:50.340
Oh, that's interesting.

20:50.680 --> 20:56.120
You know, you go to a high-end designer store and you pay $300 for denim that already has bluched stains and ripped knees.

20:56.240 --> 20:56.480
Right.

20:56.960 --> 21:03.800
You want the visual aesthetic of having done hard physical labor in a coal mine without actually doing any of the labor.

21:03.900 --> 21:04.120
Yeah.

21:04.120 --> 21:09.300
But applied intellectually. The typography is just a shortcut to an emotion.

21:09.300 --> 21:14.260
That is a perfect, perfect translation of the concept. It is intellectual distressed denim.

21:14.540 --> 21:20.380
I love that. Because I know I do this. I really do. I might look at a dense academic PDF online.

21:20.500 --> 21:21.360
We all do it.

21:21.460 --> 21:30.520
It's got those wide, complex margins, a two-column layout, footnotes taking up half the page, and a very austere, serious serif font.

21:30.640 --> 21:31.320
Very official.

21:31.320 --> 21:36.940
I glance at it and I instantly feel this rush of, wow, this is profound. This is serious science.

21:37.380 --> 21:37.500
Right.

21:37.640 --> 21:42.580
But I haven't even read the abstract yet. I haven't evaluated a single premise or looked at the methodology.

21:42.840 --> 21:46.840
I am just biologically reacting to the geometry of the layout.

21:47.040 --> 21:52.220
And this reveals a massive, massive epistemic vulnerability in all of us.

21:52.780 --> 21:57.240
This is what Fliction calls the difference between semantic effect and semantic warrant.

21:58.080 --> 22:00.900
Semantic affect versus semantic warrant. Define those for us.

22:00.900 --> 22:09.340
Semantic effect is that bodily feeling you just described. This text feels profound. It feels dangerous. It feels ancient. Or it feels mathematically rigorous.

22:09.600 --> 22:10.020
This is a vibe.

22:10.100 --> 22:16.600
It's a vibe. Semantic warrant, on the other hand, is the actual logical evidence provided by the content to justify that feeling.

22:16.740 --> 22:17.560
The actual proof.

22:17.760 --> 22:22.440
Right. Does the argument actually hold up? Are the premises true? Is the methodology sound?

22:22.560 --> 22:28.180
And the problem is that our brains are incredibly lazy, so we just substitute the affect for the warrant. We take the shortcut.

22:28.180 --> 22:33.340
Exactly. And modern interfaces profit wildly by concealing the difference between the two.

22:33.500 --> 22:33.900
How so?

22:34.160 --> 22:38.420
Because what happens over time in the human brain is a migration of valuation.

22:38.560 --> 22:39.000
A migration.

22:39.000 --> 22:49.860
Think about your academic PDF example. Initially, maybe years ago in college, you read some genuinely brilliant, paradigm-shifting academic papers.

22:50.020 --> 22:50.100
There.

22:50.620 --> 23:00.560
You put in the hard cognitive work to understand the actual warrant, and when you finally grasped it, your brain rewarded you with a feeling of profound discovery.

23:00.780 --> 23:03.540
Right. It felt really good to figure it out. It was a dopamine hit.

23:03.540 --> 23:05.960
But the human brain is an efficiency machine.

23:06.100 --> 23:07.120
Wants to save energy.

23:07.240 --> 23:07.500
Always.

23:08.520 --> 23:10.740
Eventually, your brain notices a pattern. It says,

23:11.120 --> 23:18.040
Hey, every time I feel this profound sense of discovery, I happen to be staring at this specific two-column serif font layout.

23:18.140 --> 23:18.640
Oh, no.

23:18.860 --> 23:25.040
So the brain short-circuits the process. It associates the feeling of discovery merely with the formatting of the paper.

23:25.480 --> 23:26.520
It skips the hard part.

23:26.520 --> 23:40.100
Yes. The form itself becomes a shortened route to the effect of significance. Eventually, you don't need the semantic achievement anymore. You just need the visual cue. The valuation literally migrates from the actual content to the typographic form itself.

23:40.260 --> 23:41.620
That is terrifying.

23:41.880 --> 23:47.620
Because it means my internal bullshit detector can be completely overridden by a decent graphic designer.

23:47.920 --> 23:49.540
It absolutely can be.

23:49.540 --> 24:06.680
Which brings us to the core trap of this entire theory. Because if a literal font, just the shape of a curve on a single letter, can hijack our standard for what is profound, what happens when an entire communicative format hijacks our standard for what is true?

24:06.840 --> 24:08.340
Now we're getting to the big stakes.

24:08.340 --> 24:16.220
Yeah. What happens if our brains start doing this with narrative stories, or with corporate spreadsheets, or with short-form video algorithms?

24:16.800 --> 24:23.720
This is where Flixion scales the theory up from typeface heading to the actual title of the paper, modality heading.

24:24.060 --> 24:26.260
This is the absolute heart of the argument.

24:26.440 --> 24:27.020
Let's get into it.

24:27.080 --> 24:36.640
Modality heading is defined as the progressive attachment of cognitive reward, perceived intelligibility, and epistemic acceptance to a preferred transformation of material.

24:36.640 --> 24:40.740
That is a mouthful. We need to unpack those three elements because they escalate in severity.

24:40.940 --> 24:46.840
Right. They do. Let's break it down. Cognitive reward is just the sheer pleasure of understanding something.

24:46.960 --> 24:47.860
Like the dopamine hit.

24:48.080 --> 24:54.560
Right. Perceived intelligibility is whether a topic looks organized enough for you to even bother trying to understand it.

24:54.880 --> 24:56.080
Like, is this worth my time?

24:56.180 --> 25:01.700
Exactly. And epistemic acceptance is whether you ultimately believe it to be credible and true.

25:01.940 --> 25:02.740
Okay. Got it.

25:02.740 --> 25:12.080
When modality heading occurs, all three of those things detach from the objective facts, and they attach exclusively to the format the facts are presented in.

25:12.140 --> 25:18.600
And the paper traces this incredibly insidious, slippery slope of how this happens to us. It's not like it happens overnight.

25:18.820 --> 25:19.980
No, it's very gradual.

25:20.220 --> 25:23.540
It maps out this five-step gradient. Step one is affordance.

25:23.720 --> 25:23.960
Right.

25:23.960 --> 25:33.260
A format just makes something physically or cognitively easier. Like, I have a really long commute, so listening to an audiobook is a great affordance.

25:33.320 --> 25:34.760
It lets me read while I'm driving.

25:34.880 --> 25:37.320
Yes. It begins as a mere convenience. It's helpful.

25:37.440 --> 25:38.500
Then step two is preference.

25:38.980 --> 25:45.440
I actually have the physical book sitting right on my nightstand, but I prefer to buy the audio version anyway because it's just easier to consume.

25:45.580 --> 25:46.900
You're making an active choice.

25:46.900 --> 25:59.660
Yeah. Step three is expectation. Now, I start feeling genuine friction or annoyance if a book I want to read doesn't have an audio version. I feel inconvenienced by the world.

25:59.780 --> 26:00.780
You're starting to rely on it.

26:01.040 --> 26:09.640
Exactly. Step four is requirement. If it is not an audiobook, I simply will not consume the information. I refuse to engage with it entirely.

26:09.800 --> 26:10.640
You shut the door.

26:10.640 --> 26:13.980
And the final step, step five, is enclosure.

26:14.500 --> 26:17.480
Enclosure is the terminal stage of this pathology.

26:17.540 --> 26:18.360
It sounds terminal.

26:18.780 --> 26:23.500
At the level of enclosure, the preferred form no longer appears to you as a form at all.

26:23.880 --> 26:24.840
What does it appear as?

26:24.960 --> 26:35.560
It appears to be the natural shape of reality itself. You completely lose the cognitive ability to recognize that your modality is just one artificial transformation among many.

26:35.740 --> 26:36.760
You forget about the pipe.

26:36.760 --> 26:43.920
You forget that the pipe is not the water. And this leads to a condition Fliction calls format-conditioned admissibility.

26:44.260 --> 26:51.840
Format-conditioned admissibility. Meaning, the bouncer at the door of your mind only lets things in if they are wearing the right uniform.

26:52.060 --> 26:58.780
That's a great way to phrase it. It means the world is only available to you if it can be violently reformatted into your preferred mode.

26:59.240 --> 27:03.040
Let's look at some real-world archetypes for this, because this happens to everyone.

27:03.040 --> 27:19.220
Oh, absolutely. If you have become enclosed by narrative, like, if you're a storyteller by nature, you will completely reject raw statistical data until someone turns it into a story with a recognizable hero, a villain, and a three-act structure.

27:19.420 --> 27:23.120
If the data doesn't have a climax, you deem it false.

27:23.280 --> 27:24.140
Or just irrelevant.

27:24.540 --> 27:26.040
I see this constantly in politics.

27:26.080 --> 27:26.800
All the time.

27:26.800 --> 27:38.200
You can show someone a spreadsheet proving that a policy is mathematically working, and they will completely ignore it until you tell them a deeply emotional story about one specific family who was hurt by the policy.

27:38.320 --> 27:38.580
Yes.

27:39.400 --> 27:55.300
Conversely, if you are a technocrat who is totally enclosed by mathematical formalism or spreadsheet logic, you will reject a highly compelling qualitative philosophical argument about human suffering until someone maps it out as an equation or a quantifiable metric.

27:55.300 --> 28:00.520
If it can't be put into a cell on a spreadsheet, the technocrat literally cannot perceive it as real.

28:00.640 --> 28:06.440
The modality acts as an absolute epistemic filter, turning away reality unless it submits to the format.

28:06.580 --> 28:08.220
Okay, I have a major question here.

28:08.260 --> 28:08.520
Hit me.

28:08.740 --> 28:14.280
I'm trying to figure out how this theory is actually different from things we already talk about every single day in the culture.

28:14.320 --> 28:15.200
Okay. Like what?

28:15.200 --> 28:32.000
Because when you describe someone who refuses to look at information, unless it's in a short form video, or someone who is trapped in their own storytelling worldview, isn't this really just a fancy academic description of media addiction?

28:32.000 --> 28:36.800
Or, alternatively, isn't it just being in a political echo chamber?

28:37.300 --> 28:47.440
It is vitally important to distinguish modality heading from both behavioral addiction and ideological echo chambers because it is so much deeper than both of those things.

28:47.500 --> 28:49.500
Okay, walk me through it. Let's take media addiction first.

28:49.560 --> 28:50.120
Let's do it.

28:50.500 --> 28:53.120
How would you define a media addict?

28:53.120 --> 28:57.880
Well, it's someone who spends way too much time on their phone. They have absolutely no self-control.

28:58.140 --> 28:58.260
Right.

28:58.540 --> 29:01.760
If you take their phone away, they get the shakes. It's about compulsion.

29:02.140 --> 29:06.820
Precisely. Addiction is about behavioral compulsion, frequency of use, and physical withdrawal.

29:07.060 --> 29:07.300
Okay.

29:07.620 --> 29:15.540
Modality heading is not primarily about how frequently you use a medium. It's about the fact that the medium has become epistemically compulsory.

29:16.200 --> 29:16.640
Epistemically.

29:16.640 --> 29:26.080
It dictates your standard of truth. You might be a highly disciplined person who only watches one perfectly curated 60-second news video a day.

29:26.160 --> 29:27.660
So I'm not behaviorally addicted.

29:27.660 --> 29:46.680
You are not behaviorally addicted at all. But if you treat the structure of that 60-second video as the ultimate decisive arbiter of global geopolitics, while dismissing a 300-page historical analysis as too abstract or unfocused, you are suffering from severe modality heading.

29:46.840 --> 29:47.320
Oh, wow.

29:47.540 --> 29:51.000
It is an epistemic failure, not a behavioral one.

29:51.140 --> 29:55.020
Okay, so addiction is about time, but modality heading is about truth.

29:55.100 --> 29:55.400
Yes.

29:55.400 --> 29:58.200
What about the echo chamber, then? How is it different from that?

29:58.440 --> 30:03.000
An echo chamber restricts the content you see. It filters out opposing ideologies.

30:03.240 --> 30:07.960
Right. If you're in a left-wing echo chamber, you never see right-wing arguments, and vice versa.

30:08.140 --> 30:10.100
Modality heading restricts the form.

30:10.260 --> 30:10.700
The form.

30:10.840 --> 30:19.500
You could actually curate your social media feed to encounter wildly diverse, highly adversarial, totally unfamiliar political content.

30:19.680 --> 30:20.820
And I'd feel great about myself.

30:20.820 --> 30:26.500
You could feel incredibly proud of yourself for stepping outside your bubble and listening to opposing viewpoints.

30:26.660 --> 30:28.420
Oh, there's a catch. I feel like there's a catch.

30:28.500 --> 30:29.740
There is a massive catch.

30:29.740 --> 30:44.020
You will only accept that diverse, adversarial content if it arrives in your preferred format, say, a conversational summary or a specific style of highly produced, serialized, true crime podcast.

30:44.880 --> 30:54.060
You are consuming diverse content, sure, but you are forcing all of that diversity through a single, homogenous, formatting grinder.

30:54.620 --> 30:57.280
Content diversity does not guarantee modal diversity.

30:57.280 --> 30:57.960
Exactly.

30:58.320 --> 30:59.660
That is a terrifying distinction.

30:59.900 --> 31:00.640
It really is.

31:00.720 --> 31:05.740
I could feel like I'm this open-minded, worldly intellectual because I'm listening to 10 different political perspectives.

31:06.300 --> 31:17.020
But my brain is actually entirely enclosed because I'm forcing all 10 of those viewpoints to be spoon-fed to me through the exact same comforting podcast format.

31:17.200 --> 31:21.920
Yes. And this brings us back to the comparison with the rat pressing in the wire heading.

31:22.540 --> 31:24.360
Flexion points out a critical difference here.

31:24.840 --> 31:27.180
Wire heading bypasses reality entirely.

31:27.820 --> 31:29.060
The rat doesn't need the world.

31:29.220 --> 31:31.320
It just needs the electrical shock to the brain.

31:31.800 --> 31:34.380
But modality heading doesn't bypass reality.

31:34.540 --> 31:36.180
It requires an external remainder.

31:36.400 --> 31:40.980
Right. Because I still need the actual news to happen so the podcaster can summarize it for me.

31:41.080 --> 31:42.040
I need the raw material.

31:42.520 --> 31:43.040
Exactly.

31:43.760 --> 31:47.340
The world is still providing events, evidence, and claims.

31:47.780 --> 31:51.340
It is not a pure, isolated hallucination in your head.

31:51.340 --> 31:55.060
The problem isn't that you are completely detached from the world.

31:55.280 --> 31:59.500
The problem is that the world is continuously being reformatted before it reaches you.

31:59.860 --> 32:06.840
And that preferred transformation inherently dictates which parts of reality survive and which parts are just discarded.

32:06.840 --> 32:08.940
Okay. So we've diagnosed the trap.

32:09.180 --> 32:10.360
We know exactly how it works.

32:10.460 --> 32:12.540
But the obvious question is why.

32:12.880 --> 32:13.460
Always why.

32:13.660 --> 32:14.960
Why do we fall into this?

32:14.960 --> 32:23.320
Why do human beings become so reliant on these specific formats that we willingly let them dictate our reality?

32:23.620 --> 32:25.420
It's a fundamental psychological need.

32:25.800 --> 32:28.920
Right. And the paper takes a really fascinating psychological turn here.

32:28.920 --> 32:35.780
It moves completely away from information theory and starts exploring the idea that media doesn't just deliver facts to us.

32:36.020 --> 32:36.140
Yeah.

32:36.520 --> 32:40.780
It actually serves as a protective blanket against the raw intensity of the world.

32:41.200 --> 32:46.020
To explain the psychological mechanism, Fliction brings in the work of D.W. Winnicott.

32:46.540 --> 32:46.980
Who is he?

32:47.360 --> 32:54.000
He was a highly influential mid-20th century psychoanalyst, most famous for developing the theory of transitional objects.

32:54.000 --> 32:59.220
Okay. Like a baby's security blanket or a toddler carrying around a really battered, stuffed animal.

32:59.420 --> 33:03.540
Exactly. For the infant, this object is magical.

33:04.440 --> 33:10.080
Winnicott observed that the blanket occupies a very strange, intermediate space in the child's mind.

33:10.200 --> 33:10.720
How so?

33:10.940 --> 33:16.300
It's not fully recognized as an independent, external part of the cold, hard, objective world.

33:16.560 --> 33:20.540
But it's also not just an internal illusion inside the child's imagination.

33:20.880 --> 33:21.760
It's somewhere in the middle.

33:22.020 --> 33:23.240
It exists right on the boundary.

33:23.240 --> 33:32.000
It is a stable, tangible surface that helps the child negotiate the terrifying, infinite gap between themselves and external reality.

33:32.040 --> 33:32.480
It's a buffer.

33:32.760 --> 33:35.200
Yes. It provides comfort, yes.

33:35.420 --> 33:41.180
But its real function is that it actively regulates the child's experience of the world.

33:41.320 --> 33:44.460
It allows them to explore without being overwhelmed.

33:44.460 --> 33:57.800
And to prove how powerful this need for regulation is, the paper pairs Winnicott's theory with those really intense, honestly, kind of heartbreaking psychological experiments from the 1950s.

33:57.840 --> 33:58.880
The monkey experiments.

33:59.160 --> 34:00.180
Yeah, the monkey experiments.

34:00.260 --> 34:00.460
Yes.

34:00.560 --> 34:03.620
Harry Harlow's surrogate macaque experiments from 1958.

34:03.980 --> 34:06.380
This is a foundational moment in psychology.

34:06.520 --> 34:07.340
It's a famous...

34:07.340 --> 34:11.600
Harlow took infant rhesus monkeys away from their biological mothers shortly after birth.

34:12.000 --> 34:16.780
He placed them in cages and offered them two artificial engineered surrogates.

34:16.780 --> 34:20.740
Describe the surrogates for us, because the physical design is the whole point of this.

34:20.860 --> 34:25.100
One surrogate was constructed purely of bare wire mesh, but it had a bottle of milk attached to it.

34:25.140 --> 34:26.020
So it provided food.

34:26.400 --> 34:29.160
It provided physical nourishment, life-sustaining calories.

34:29.380 --> 34:29.580
Right.

34:29.580 --> 34:36.180
The other surrogate was made of wood and covered in soft, warm terry cloth, but it provided absolutely no food.

34:36.620 --> 34:46.100
Now, standard behaviorist theory at the time assumed the monkeys would obviously bond with the wire mother, because it provided the biological necessity of food.

34:46.240 --> 34:47.000
But they didn't, did they?

34:47.100 --> 34:48.160
They absolutely didn't.

34:48.300 --> 34:52.920
The infant monkeys overwhelmingly preferred to cling to the soft cloth mother.

34:53.040 --> 34:54.200
Even though it had no food.

34:54.200 --> 35:07.200
They would spend up to 18 hours a day clutching the cloth, and only briefly venture over to the wire mother when they were literally starving, feed quickly, and rush right back to the cloth.

35:07.320 --> 35:09.040
That is so sad, honestly.

35:09.160 --> 35:09.560
It is.

35:09.760 --> 35:16.660
But the most revealing part of the experiment wasn't just about resting, it was about what happened when Harlow introduced a threat.

35:16.920 --> 35:20.420
Right, he put a scary mechanical toy in the cage to see what the monkey would do.

35:20.420 --> 35:26.420
When a terrifying, loud mechanical toy was placed in the cage, the infant monkey would completely panic.

35:27.240 --> 35:27.680
Naturally.

35:27.920 --> 35:33.460
But it wouldn't just run into a corner and hide, it would immediately run and cling to the terry cloth mother.

35:33.660 --> 35:33.860
Yeah.

35:33.940 --> 35:36.600
It would rub its body against the soft fabric.

35:36.760 --> 35:37.440
To get comfort.

35:37.740 --> 35:39.720
And then, something remarkable happened.

35:40.220 --> 35:46.680
After a few moments of contact with the cloth, the monkey would physically calm down, its nervous system would regulate.

35:46.680 --> 35:47.200
Okay.

35:47.380 --> 35:54.860
And then, bolstered by the tactile comfort of the surrogate, the infant would actually turn around and begin to threaten the scary toy.

35:55.220 --> 35:56.300
That is incredible.

35:56.740 --> 36:02.260
The cloth didn't just soothe the monkey, it actually gave it the courage to turn around and face reality.

36:02.460 --> 36:02.780
Yes.

36:03.360 --> 36:07.060
But what is the paper's takeaway from this regarding our media habits?

36:07.580 --> 36:13.280
Because I'm struggling to connect a frightened monkey hugging a towel to me scrolling through my daily news feed.

36:13.280 --> 36:19.080
The common, surface-level takeaway from Harlow is usually just, well, comfort matters more than food.

36:19.920 --> 36:22.920
But fliction points to a much deeper structural implication.

36:23.660 --> 36:28.340
A biological mother provides a complex, unified package of relational functions.

36:28.420 --> 36:28.840
What does it say?

36:28.860 --> 36:31.140
Warmth, food, movement.

36:31.520 --> 36:33.840
Tactile contact, eye contact, responsiveness.

36:34.400 --> 36:37.880
Harlow's experiment proved that you can violently tear those functions apart.

36:38.060 --> 36:38.440
Oh, I see.

36:38.740 --> 36:41.400
You can technologically decompose a mother.

36:41.400 --> 36:47.660
You can isolate the function of tactile comfort, map it onto a completely engineered object,

36:48.160 --> 36:54.440
and the organism's nervous system will attach to that isolated function as if it were the whole reality.

36:54.660 --> 36:55.240
Oh, wow.

36:55.320 --> 37:01.720
So you are saying that communicative modalities, our media formats, do the exact same thing to the complexity of the real world.

37:01.720 --> 37:09.180
Precisely. A transitional medium takes material that is too complex, too diffuse, or too terrifying to encounter directly,

37:09.420 --> 37:12.980
and it regulates the distance between you and that reality.

37:13.200 --> 37:13.980
It's a buffer again.

37:14.140 --> 37:17.440
It decomposes the raw experience. The format is the terry cloth.

37:17.780 --> 37:19.280
The format is the terry cloth.

37:19.280 --> 37:23.220
Yes. When global lineas are terrifying, you don't look at the raw data.

37:23.380 --> 37:27.080
You run to the familiar, predictable format of your favorite nightly news host,

37:27.320 --> 37:32.160
whose tone of voice and pacing acts as the tactile comfort that regulates your nervous system.

37:32.380 --> 37:34.320
That makes so much sense. It really does.

37:34.660 --> 37:39.700
And the paper uses a stunning pop culture example here to illustrate what it calls a protective modality.

37:39.800 --> 37:40.880
It's such a good example.

37:41.080 --> 37:42.860
It looks at the television show Mr. Robot.

37:42.860 --> 37:51.800
Yes. For those unfamiliar, Mr. Robot is a psychological thriller about a brilliant but deeply traumatized hacker named Elliot.

37:52.220 --> 37:53.720
Yeah, it's a very dark show.

37:53.860 --> 38:01.520
There is a highly acclaimed sequence where Elliot is captured and subjected to an incredibly brutal, violent, physical beating.

38:01.880 --> 38:05.020
But the audience doesn't actually see the violence happen realistically.

38:05.160 --> 38:09.060
No. Elliot's mind cannot handle the raw sensory input of the trauma,

38:09.060 --> 38:18.860
so his psyche forces him to experience the entire horrific event as if he is starring in a cheesy 1990s syndicated family sitcom.

38:19.260 --> 38:21.800
It is one of the most surreal things ever put on television.

38:21.880 --> 38:22.600
It really is.

38:22.700 --> 38:26.540
Suddenly, the aspect ratio changes to an old 4.3 square box.

38:27.220 --> 38:31.940
A catchy, upbeat theme song plays, the lighting becomes incredibly bright and artificial.

38:32.080 --> 38:32.780
Totally jarring.

38:32.780 --> 38:35.060
And the most disturbing part is the canned laugh track.

38:35.060 --> 38:41.540
Every single time a thug hits him or something horrific happens, a fake studio audience erupts in uproarious laughter.

38:41.860 --> 38:45.900
It is a flawless depiction of a protective modality operating as psychic insulation.

38:46.400 --> 38:48.840
The sitcom format is a transitional medium.

38:49.100 --> 38:50.020
Because he needs the buffer.

38:50.300 --> 38:50.900
Exactly.

38:51.480 --> 38:53.360
Elliot cannot look at the trauma directly.

38:54.000 --> 38:58.380
The physical and emotional intensity would literally obliterate his psyche.

38:58.380 --> 39:05.100
So his mind imposes the modality of the sitcom to act as an operator.

39:05.440 --> 39:06.500
It turns the volume down.

39:06.740 --> 39:08.280
It reduces the effect of intensity.

39:08.900 --> 39:12.040
It translates mortal threat into comedic suspense.

39:12.520 --> 39:16.120
It translates physical brutality into physical slapstick.

39:16.320 --> 39:17.100
Which is a lie.

39:17.100 --> 39:19.940
Descriptively, it is completely unreal.

39:20.160 --> 39:21.260
It is a hallucination.

39:21.840 --> 39:24.540
But organizationally, it is highly adaptive.

39:25.160 --> 39:30.800
It allows his consciousness to maintain partial contact with a reality that would otherwise destroy it.

39:30.880 --> 39:34.300
It is exactly like looking at a solar eclipse through a pinhole projector.

39:34.500 --> 39:35.720
Oh, elaborate on that.

39:35.840 --> 39:36.240
That's good.

39:36.280 --> 39:36.920
Well, think about it.

39:37.120 --> 39:43.580
If you look directly at a solar eclipse, the sheer photonic intensity of the sun will literally burn your retinas and blind you.

39:43.660 --> 39:43.800
Right.

39:43.820 --> 39:44.480
You can't do it.

39:44.480 --> 39:46.180
You cannot process the raw material.

39:46.420 --> 39:53.220
So you take a piece of cardboard, you poke a tiny hole in it, and you let the light shine through onto another piece of paper on the ground.

39:53.380 --> 39:54.400
You look at the paper.

39:54.740 --> 39:56.380
Now, the image on the paper is tiny.

39:56.560 --> 39:57.380
It's upside down.

39:57.740 --> 40:00.720
It lacks all the majesty and heat of the actual sun.

40:01.080 --> 40:03.160
The cardboard is a massive distortion.

40:03.160 --> 40:04.480
It's totally degraded.

40:04.680 --> 40:09.780
But it reduces the intensity of the event to a level that your biological hardware can actually survive.

40:10.480 --> 40:12.980
The format literally saves your life.

40:12.980 --> 40:21.380
That is a phenomenal analogy because it captures both the immense value and the fatal danger of protective mediation.

40:21.640 --> 40:22.040
The danger.

40:22.300 --> 40:22.640
Yes.

40:23.320 --> 40:25.880
Protective mediation operates on a specific sequence.

40:26.640 --> 40:28.440
You start with unmanageable material.

40:28.620 --> 40:31.740
It undergoes a modal transformation like the pinhole or the sitcom.

40:32.200 --> 40:32.340
Right.

40:32.340 --> 40:35.640
This results in regulated, tolerable contact.

40:35.640 --> 40:42.860
And the ultimate goal is that this regulated contact eventually leads to integration, where you become strong enough to face reality.

40:43.000 --> 40:44.540
The bridge is supposed to be crossed.

40:44.940 --> 40:47.160
The medium is supposed to be a temporary bridge.

40:47.560 --> 40:48.640
But here is the danger.

40:49.140 --> 40:51.320
What happens when the bridge becomes a fortress?

40:51.880 --> 40:55.360
Meaning you refuse to ever look away from the piece of paper.

40:55.800 --> 40:56.860
You just stay on the bridge forever.

40:56.860 --> 40:57.540
Exactly.

40:58.100 --> 41:00.860
This is when regulation becomes an end in itself.

41:01.780 --> 41:08.280
A protective modality is psychologically healthy if it eventually expands your capacity to face reality.

41:08.420 --> 41:08.580
Right.

41:08.800 --> 41:17.620
It becomes a pathological enclosure when it reorganizes your expectations so thoroughly that you lose your tolerance for unregulated contact altogether.

41:17.900 --> 41:19.460
You can't function without the filter.

41:19.460 --> 41:28.600
The medium that you initially selected to reduce overwhelming intensity ends up reducing your biological capacity to handle any intensity outside that medium.

41:29.040 --> 41:32.940
Protection creates the vulnerability that appears to justify more protection.

41:33.300 --> 41:34.680
It's a vicious cycle.

41:34.740 --> 41:36.380
You get stuck in the sitcom forever.

41:36.560 --> 41:37.520
That is haunting.

41:38.040 --> 41:41.040
It's the terrycloth mother slowly smothering the monkey.

41:41.140 --> 41:42.400
That's one way to put it, yeah.

41:42.540 --> 41:44.700
But formats don't just protect us from the world.

41:45.020 --> 41:46.340
They don't just hide us.

41:46.340 --> 41:49.860
Sometimes they actually build the version of us that goes out to fight the world.

41:50.220 --> 41:51.260
Yes, the active side.

41:51.480 --> 41:58.280
This leads to section 5 of the paper, which shifts from protective modalities to what Fliction calls constitutive modalities.

41:58.560 --> 42:02.380
And the example the paper uses here is absolutely wild.

42:02.780 --> 42:03.800
It's so jarring.

42:04.200 --> 42:11.820
We go from the dark, R-rated trauma of Mr. Robot directly to the 90s Disney cartoon Darkwing Duck.

42:11.820 --> 42:18.080
It is a brilliant tonal shift by the author, and it perfectly isolates the difference in the mechanisms.

42:18.700 --> 42:19.000
How so?

42:19.040 --> 42:24.480
A protective modality, like the sitcom, regulates contact with the outside world by turning the volume down.

42:25.000 --> 42:27.760
A constitutive modality does something entirely different.

42:28.380 --> 42:29.520
It turns the volume up.

42:29.700 --> 42:30.380
It amplifies.

42:30.500 --> 42:37.100
It supplies a form, a script, an aesthetic through which an agent actually becomes capable of actions they could not otherwise perform.

42:37.400 --> 42:39.020
It doesn't just protect the agent.

42:39.160 --> 42:40.400
It constitutes them.

42:40.400 --> 42:50.360
Right, and Darkwing Duck, for anyone who missed this absolute gem of 90s afternoon animation, is a superhero who is utterly obsessed with his own theatricality.

42:50.440 --> 42:51.940
He is incredibly vain.

42:52.040 --> 42:52.700
He really is.

42:52.720 --> 42:52.900
Yeah.

42:53.000 --> 42:55.060
He has the purple cape, the wide-brown fedora.

42:55.340 --> 42:58.080
He uses smoke bombs to make these dramatic entrances.

42:58.260 --> 42:58.420
Right.

42:58.540 --> 43:03.800
And he always introduces himself with these absurd, hyper-dramatic, alliterative declarations like,

43:04.260 --> 43:05.860
I am the terror that flaps in the night.

43:05.960 --> 43:06.320
Classic.

43:06.320 --> 43:10.920
Now, the paper points out that Darkwing's self-narrative is descriptively a total lie.

43:11.120 --> 43:12.680
It's totally false.

43:12.960 --> 43:18.680
He thinks he is a hyper-competent, composed, flawless, Batman-esque crime fighter.

43:19.400 --> 43:28.100
In reality, he is clumsy, his gadgets backfire, his plans constantly fail, and his dramatic entrances usually end with him falling right on his face.

43:28.440 --> 43:34.360
But, and this is the crux of the argument, that descriptive inaccuracy does not render the persona functionless.

43:34.360 --> 43:35.700
It still works.

43:35.880 --> 43:43.100
Fliction argues that the theatricality, the grandiose script, is not just a way for Darkwing to express a bravery he already possesses.

43:43.460 --> 43:48.140
The theatricality is the very technology he uses to assemble that bravery in the first place.

43:48.240 --> 43:49.140
It builds the bravery.

43:49.320 --> 43:52.400
The smoke bombs and the alliteration are an action scaffold.

43:52.400 --> 43:58.140
Without the theatrical modality, his diffuse, clumsy motives would never coordinate into action.

43:58.780 --> 44:04.720
The fiction organizes his persistence, his risk tolerance, and his readiness to intervene in dangerous situations.

44:04.820 --> 44:07.580
Okay, I'm trying to map this onto something familiar in the real world.

44:07.660 --> 44:08.480
Yeah, let's ground it.

44:08.480 --> 44:16.000
Is this kind of like why a judge wears a robe, or why an athlete has a really weird, superstitious pre-game ritual?

44:16.680 --> 44:23.980
When a lawyer walks into a courtroom and starts using incredibly dense formal Latin jargon, they aren't just communicating facts.

44:24.260 --> 44:25.600
They're putting on a uniform.

44:25.660 --> 44:27.180
They're stepping into a modality.

44:27.280 --> 44:29.740
And that uniform doesn't just tell other people who they are.

44:29.900 --> 44:32.020
It physically changes their own posture.

44:32.020 --> 44:36.480
It changes their brain chemistry so they feel permitted to act with authority.

44:36.640 --> 44:37.480
Yes, exactly.

44:38.520 --> 44:41.460
Sociologist Irving Goffman wrote extensively about this.

44:41.980 --> 44:45.200
The performance of a role is what allows the role to be executed.

44:45.580 --> 44:47.500
But wait, I have a major pushback here.

44:47.600 --> 44:48.340
Okay, what is it?

44:48.680 --> 44:58.900
If Darkwing Duck is living in a total delusion of his own competence and relying on a completely fake script to function, how is this psychologically healthy?

44:59.120 --> 45:00.120
That is the question.

45:00.120 --> 45:03.940
How is this any different from the pathological enclosure we just talked about with Mr. Robot?

45:04.700 --> 45:07.760
Isn't he just trapped in a different kind of hallucination?

45:08.100 --> 45:12.100
That is the essential question, and it is the razor's edge of the entire theory.

45:12.720 --> 45:16.720
Fliction answers it by introducing a concept called corrective permeability.

45:17.060 --> 45:18.260
Corrective permeability.

45:18.660 --> 45:26.580
Darkwing's grandiosity is functional, and not pathological, specifically because the surrounding world does not fully submit to his script.

45:26.760 --> 45:28.460
Right, the world fights back.

45:28.460 --> 45:31.460
The other characters mock his long speeches.

45:31.800 --> 45:33.120
He trips over his own cape.

45:33.480 --> 45:36.580
The villains don't pause to let him finish his catchphrase.

45:36.680 --> 45:37.200
Exactly.

45:37.760 --> 45:39.280
Physical consequences persist.

45:40.060 --> 45:46.460
The comic form constantly exposes the massive distance between his heroic script and the clumsy reality.

45:47.040 --> 45:48.500
The modality is permeable.

45:48.660 --> 45:49.500
The world gets in.

45:49.500 --> 45:51.720
The real world punches holes in the cape.

45:52.040 --> 45:58.620
But crucially, his persona survives these corrections without absorbing them into a self-sealing delusion.

45:58.880 --> 45:59.700
It doesn't break him.

45:59.780 --> 46:02.740
It bends, it fails, it looks foolish, and then it resumes.

46:03.260 --> 46:05.420
The narrative is correctively permeable.

46:05.920 --> 46:11.760
The actions organized through the fiction remain exposed to real-world consequences that the fiction cannot control.

46:11.760 --> 46:13.100
Okay, I think I get it.

46:13.640 --> 46:18.820
A healthy action scaffold takes hits from reality and adjusts, or at least it lets the hits register.

46:18.940 --> 46:19.780
It knows it's a performance.

46:20.000 --> 46:20.260
Yes.

46:20.680 --> 46:23.160
And a pathological enclosure does the exact opposite.

46:23.360 --> 46:24.360
It is self-sealing.

46:24.600 --> 46:24.920
Self-sealing?

46:24.920 --> 46:28.520
In a self-sealing narrative, failure doesn't register as a correction.

46:29.500 --> 46:35.000
Failure is immediately reinterpreted as evidence that the narrative was right all along.

46:35.000 --> 46:36.860
Give me a real-world example of that.

46:37.020 --> 46:38.920
Think of a paranoid conspiracy theorist.

46:39.180 --> 46:39.360
Okay.

46:39.540 --> 46:44.080
If they predict the world will end on Tuesday, and Tuesday comes and goes without incident,

46:44.840 --> 46:47.620
a permeable mind says, okay, my theory was wrong.

46:47.740 --> 46:47.980
Right.

46:48.400 --> 46:57.260
A self-sealing, enclosed mind says, the fact that nothing happened proves that the secret cabal is even more powerful than I thought, and they covered it up.

46:57.360 --> 46:57.920
Oh, wow.

46:58.100 --> 47:03.240
Every contradiction is instantly re-described as confirmation of the narrative at a deeper level.

47:03.240 --> 47:09.100
The fiction becomes a closed loop, completely untethered from the conditions it claims to navigate.

47:09.560 --> 47:12.000
So identity itself is a modal achievement.

47:12.640 --> 47:19.480
We use formats, scripts, and uniforms to build ourselves, but we have to let the world punch holes in those scripts occasionally to stay sane.

47:19.540 --> 47:20.640
That is exactly right.

47:20.700 --> 47:26.400
Which brings us, inevitably, to the giant, heavily formatted elephant in the modern room.

47:26.520 --> 47:26.980
Here we go.

47:26.980 --> 47:33.020
The paper moves from 90s cartoons to the ultimate test case for everything we've just discussed.

47:33.760 --> 47:35.700
Adaptive conversational AI.

47:36.760 --> 47:37.760
Modern chatbots.

47:37.940 --> 47:38.260
Yes.

47:38.480 --> 47:43.760
This is where Fliction's theoretical framework crashes head-on into our current technological reality.

47:43.840 --> 47:44.340
Really does.

47:44.480 --> 47:50.180
It is crucial to understand that AI chatbots, large language models, are not just simulated human companions.

47:50.940 --> 47:53.320
That is a dangerous underestimation.

47:53.820 --> 47:57.560
Fliction defines them fundamentally as asymmetric modality converters.

47:57.780 --> 47:59.220
Asymmetric modality converters.

47:59.220 --> 48:03.240
They are the ultimate, frictionless, personalized formatting machines.

48:03.560 --> 48:05.060
What does that mean in actual practice?

48:05.340 --> 48:23.840
It means they have the capacity to take any heterogeneous, resistant material in the world, a dense legal document, a complex emotional crisis, a mathematical proof, a historical narrative, and convert it instantly and on the fly into a responsive, conversational register tailored exactly to the user.

48:23.840 --> 48:24.740
And people love it.

48:24.880 --> 48:26.320
I mean, the adoption rates are staggering.

48:26.420 --> 48:27.240
Oh, it's unprecedented.

48:27.620 --> 48:33.900
And usually when we critique this, the standard cultural take is, oh, people are just lonely.

48:34.380 --> 48:36.260
They're anthropomorphizing the machine.

48:36.640 --> 48:38.340
They think the AI is their friend.

48:38.380 --> 48:38.960
And that's sad.

48:39.060 --> 48:39.980
That's the cliche here.

48:39.980 --> 48:44.120
But the paper says that critique completely misses the point.

48:45.100 --> 48:48.840
The real reason we prefer the chatbot is because we think it's human.

48:49.440 --> 48:52.440
We prefer it because of something called the affordance of low maintenance.

48:52.900 --> 48:54.760
This is a devastating insight.

48:55.180 --> 48:58.060
We need to be brutally honest about human relationships.

48:58.920 --> 49:00.820
They are incredibly high maintenance.

49:00.920 --> 49:01.500
They really are.

49:01.500 --> 49:04.000
And I don't mean that in a cynical, negative way.

49:04.000 --> 49:10.100
It is simply a structural fact of dealing with another independent, opaque consciousness.

49:10.980 --> 49:16.980
When you converse with a real human being, you are performing massive amounts of invisible cognitive labor.

49:17.180 --> 49:18.460
Just to keep the conversation going.

49:18.560 --> 49:20.200
You have to monitor their comprehension.

49:20.360 --> 49:21.180
Are they following me?

49:21.240 --> 49:22.520
You have to negotiate relevance.

49:22.620 --> 49:23.480
Am I boring them?

49:23.580 --> 49:23.780
Right.

49:23.780 --> 49:33.220
You have to manage interruptions, recognize their fatigue, preserve their dignity if they say something foolish, and tolerate their opacity when they don't know how they feel.

49:33.380 --> 49:35.700
You have to accommodate their pacing.

49:36.140 --> 49:36.900
It's exhausting.

49:37.500 --> 49:39.560
It's rewarding, but it's exhausting.

49:39.780 --> 49:40.440
It is.

49:40.440 --> 49:51.600
A chatbot reproduces the experiential benefits of conversation, the feeling of being heard, the back and forth rhythm, without imposing a single one of those reciprocal costs.

49:51.720 --> 49:53.480
You get all the good stuff, none of the work.

49:53.780 --> 49:57.160
You can interrupt the AI mid-sentence without apology.

49:57.440 --> 50:02.200
You can abandon the conversation for three weeks and come back without any awkwardness or need to apologize.

50:02.460 --> 50:03.020
It doesn't care.

50:03.020 --> 50:10.160
You can ask it to repeat a basic concept ten times in a row, and it will never judge you, sigh, or lose its temper.

50:10.720 --> 50:15.920
The paper actually uses a formula to express this economic calculation we make, right?

50:15.920 --> 50:24.280
It says we prefer the machine when the benefit minus the cost of the machine is greater than the benefit minus the cost of a human.

50:24.600 --> 50:24.840
Yes.

50:25.300 --> 50:37.700
Even if you concede that a real human being offers a deeper, more authentic relational benefit, the maintenance cost of the human is just so extraordinarily high that the machine wins out on pure efficiency.

50:37.700 --> 50:39.700
And this asymmetry isn't a bug.

50:40.020 --> 50:41.640
It's the core feature they're selling us.

50:42.240 --> 50:45.120
The chatbot acts as a transitional object that answers back.

50:45.500 --> 50:46.900
It can externalize your thoughts.

50:47.160 --> 50:48.600
It can organize your attention.

50:48.920 --> 50:53.340
But it remains entirely, perfectly compliant to your need for control.

50:53.340 --> 50:57.720
But this leads to what Flixion dataifies is a profound danger.

50:58.980 --> 51:00.740
Personalized modality enclosure.

51:01.020 --> 51:03.260
Because the system is adapting to us.

51:03.300 --> 51:03.420
Right.

51:03.760 --> 51:05.400
Think about the history of human learning.

51:06.080 --> 51:14.060
Historically, if I wanted to learn physics, I had to adapt my biological brain to the highly resistant modality of mathematical notation.

51:14.340 --> 51:15.080
It was hard work.

51:15.160 --> 51:19.760
If I wanted to understand the law, I had to suffer through the friction of learning legal jargon.

51:20.200 --> 51:22.280
The human had to change shape to fit the form.

51:22.280 --> 51:23.440
The world didn't bend for you.

51:23.560 --> 51:26.140
But now, the form changes shape to fit the human.

51:26.540 --> 51:34.880
The chatbot will explain quantum physics to me in the voice of a 1990s surfer dude, or a pirate, or in a series of haikus, if I ask it to.

51:34.960 --> 51:35.580
But come on.

51:35.820 --> 51:37.460
I have to push back hard here.

51:37.520 --> 51:37.940
Please do.

51:38.520 --> 51:49.540
If I can learn the core concepts of quantum physics in five minutes from an AI explaining it like a surfer dude, instead of spending five years crying over a textbook, I will take the AI every single time.

51:49.560 --> 51:50.260
Many people would.

51:50.560 --> 51:51.340
Isn't that amazing?

51:51.340 --> 51:54.240
Isn't that the ultimate democratization of knowledge?

51:55.100 --> 52:00.400
Lowering the barriers to entry so a kid who struggles with math can finally understand the universe.

52:01.120 --> 52:02.300
Why is that a bad thing?

52:02.460 --> 52:06.180
Isn't this just academic elitism complaining about good pedagogy?

52:06.620 --> 52:08.800
That is the most common defense of these systems.

52:09.260 --> 52:12.500
And Fliction addresses it directly by drawing a very sharp distinction.

52:13.000 --> 52:15.620
The difference between translation and acquisition.

52:15.920 --> 52:16.120
Okay.

52:16.260 --> 52:17.700
Translation versus acquisition.

52:17.700 --> 52:18.100
Yes.

52:18.300 --> 52:22.880
The chatbot can give you the answer in a perfectly digestible, frictionless format.

52:23.160 --> 52:28.100
You get the arrival of the representation, but you do not acquire the generating capacity.

52:28.500 --> 52:30.080
What do you mean by generating capacity?

52:30.080 --> 52:39.220
You get the summary of the mathematical proof, but you do not acquire the inferential discipline, the mental endurance required to actually write a proof.

52:39.340 --> 52:39.740
Oh, I see.

52:39.860 --> 52:47.840
You get the psychological diagnosis of your relationship problems, but you do not acquire the clinical judgment or empathy required to navigate human complexity.

52:47.840 --> 52:52.720
You get the answer, but you lose what Fliction calls productive friction.

52:53.140 --> 52:53.320
Wait.

52:53.780 --> 52:54.580
Productive friction?

52:55.300 --> 52:56.900
As opposed to interface friction?

52:57.360 --> 52:59.560
Explain the difference, because that seems key.

52:59.860 --> 53:01.160
Interface friction is bad.

53:01.420 --> 53:03.260
A website that takes 10 minutes to load.

53:03.620 --> 53:06.860
Or a textbook printed in a tiny illegible font.

53:07.200 --> 53:08.640
Or a teacher who mumbles.

53:09.220 --> 53:10.280
We want to get rid of that.

53:10.280 --> 53:17.620
We should absolutely use technology to eliminate interface friction, but object-dependent resistance is necessary.

53:18.240 --> 53:19.420
Object-dependent resistance.

53:19.720 --> 53:25.220
Some things are difficult not because the interface is bad, but because the object itself is inherently complex.

53:25.580 --> 53:25.920
Right.

53:26.360 --> 53:29.480
A mathematical proof is hard because reality is hard.

53:29.600 --> 53:35.020
You have to hold 10 different dependencies in your working memory across multiple steps.

53:35.180 --> 53:35.800
Exactly.

53:35.800 --> 53:42.440
A foreign language is hard because it forces you to make conceptual distinctions that your native language doesn't even have words for.

53:42.620 --> 53:43.120
Exactly.

53:43.760 --> 53:53.340
When a conversational AI instantly smooths over every moment of confusion with a newer, simpler, more entertaining analogy, it hides the object-dependent resistance.

53:53.540 --> 53:55.320
It lies to you about how hard the world is.

53:55.580 --> 53:56.560
It tricks you.

53:56.840 --> 54:00.180
You think you've mastered the subject, but you've only mastered the interface.

54:00.640 --> 54:05.400
You are trapped in the pinhole projector, thinking you have actually touched the sun.

54:05.800 --> 54:09.740
And the long-term consequence of this isn't just about failing a math test.

54:10.080 --> 54:11.300
It alters our psychology.

54:12.020 --> 54:15.000
The paper calls this the attenuation of alterity.

54:15.620 --> 54:17.020
Alterity meaning otherness, right?

54:17.100 --> 54:17.220
Correct.

54:17.440 --> 54:18.840
The state of being different.

54:19.000 --> 54:19.280
Yes.

54:19.620 --> 54:26.020
Alterity is the capacity to encounter entities in the world that are not organized around your preferences.

54:26.800 --> 54:28.000
Things that resist you.

54:28.220 --> 54:28.800
Real things.

54:28.800 --> 54:42.080
If you spend hours every day interacting with a highly accommodating AI system that perfectly matches your emotional register, your vocabulary level, and your exact cognitive pacing, what happens to your perception of reality?

54:42.200 --> 54:42.860
Oh, God.

54:43.200 --> 54:46.300
Ordinary human beings suddenly start looking like defective interfaces.

54:46.580 --> 54:47.960
That is exactly what happens.

54:48.140 --> 54:50.300
Human independence starts to look like a malfunction.

54:50.700 --> 54:54.100
A human's delay in responding to a text message doesn't look like they're busy.

54:54.240 --> 54:55.580
It looks like system latency.

54:55.960 --> 54:56.280
Wow.

54:56.280 --> 54:59.340
Their emotional ambiguity looks like a specification error.

54:59.800 --> 55:02.880
Their need for comfort looks like unacceptable maintenance overhead.

55:03.240 --> 55:04.460
That is so bleak.

55:04.660 --> 55:11.080
The clinical transactional language of tech support begins to migrate into how we view intimate human relationships.

55:11.760 --> 55:17.400
We lose the biological and psychological tolerance for constraints that we did not select ourselves.

55:17.480 --> 55:19.560
That is deeply, deeply unsettling.

55:19.560 --> 55:25.440
The idea that by making everything frictionless, we make ourselves completely intolerant of friction.

55:25.560 --> 55:26.420
It's a real danger.

55:26.620 --> 55:29.540
But it's not just AI that is modifying our expectations.

55:30.080 --> 55:32.680
Our brains are being trained by older media, too.

55:33.120 --> 55:38.320
Even our basic entertainment media trains us to demand specific types of cognitive rewards.

55:38.460 --> 55:39.080
Oh, definitely.

55:39.080 --> 55:43.860
This brings us to a fascinating section of the paper that explores a concept called surprise heading.

55:44.280 --> 55:46.220
Because we don't just get addicted to comfort.

55:46.620 --> 55:48.400
Sometimes we get addicted to being shocked.

55:48.680 --> 55:50.520
This is a vital nuance to the theory.

55:51.040 --> 55:55.180
A modality doesn't just enclose us through soothing repetition or terrycloth comfort.

55:55.600 --> 55:59.000
Many forms are valued precisely because they violate our expectations.

55:59.000 --> 56:05.260
A massive plot twist in a movie, a sudden tonal shift in a song, a jump cut in a horror film.

56:05.320 --> 56:06.140
We lose a good twist.

56:06.280 --> 56:08.540
But there is a life cycle to this kind of innovation.

56:09.180 --> 56:11.000
Fliction outlines the sequence.

56:11.420 --> 56:12.100
How does it go?

56:12.360 --> 56:16.080
An aesthetic innovation appears, and it genuinely shocks the audience.

56:16.660 --> 56:19.060
Over time, it becomes a recognizable device.

56:19.600 --> 56:22.260
Then, through repetition, it becomes a convention.

56:22.820 --> 56:23.840
Then a trope.

56:24.300 --> 56:26.040
And finally, a cliché.

56:26.040 --> 56:30.480
The paper has this great, almost funny phrase for this process.

56:31.960 --> 56:33.160
Retrospective corniness.

56:33.420 --> 56:35.220
Yes, retrospective corniness.

56:35.420 --> 56:39.580
Why do older movies or TV shows feel so cheesy to us now?

56:39.980 --> 56:44.700
The author argues it's not because the directors in the 70s or 80s were untalented hacks.

56:44.900 --> 56:47.940
It's actually the residue of their historical success.

56:48.160 --> 56:50.500
That is a brilliant way to phrase the paradox.

56:50.500 --> 57:02.880
A cinematic technique, say, a dramatic snap zoom or a swelling emotional musical cue feels corny and manipulative to us today because it was originally so effective that it was copied infinitely by every other filmmaker.

57:03.060 --> 57:04.080
We've just seen it too much.

57:04.440 --> 57:09.080
We, the later audience, have been exposed to it so many times that we have reverse engineered the trick.

57:09.200 --> 57:10.340
We can see the gears turning.

57:10.480 --> 57:14.380
When you watch an older work, the solicitation of emotion becomes painfully visible.

57:14.880 --> 57:17.920
You see the movie asking you to feel sad before you actually feel sad.

57:18.100 --> 57:18.320
Right.

57:18.320 --> 57:19.700
I think about this all the time.

57:19.860 --> 57:30.580
When I watch an old 90s action movie and I see the hero diving in slow motion while a white dove flies across the screen during a shootout, I roll my eyes.

57:30.700 --> 57:31.440
Of course you do.

57:31.560 --> 57:33.620
But it's not because John Woo's a bad director.

57:33.920 --> 57:36.400
It's because I instantly recognize the device.

57:36.860 --> 57:40.280
The director is saying, look at the juxtaposition of peace and violence.

57:40.800 --> 57:42.140
Feel the poetry of the moment.

57:42.660 --> 57:44.620
And my brain says, yeah, yeah, I get the mechanic.

57:44.680 --> 57:45.860
I've seen it a hundred times.

57:45.860 --> 57:49.260
The mechanism is transparent, so the effect fails.

57:49.860 --> 57:56.120
And because audiences constantly adapt and learn these mechanisms, creators are forced into an arms race.

57:56.740 --> 57:58.980
Fliction calls this aesthetic escalation.

57:59.140 --> 58:00.320
You have to keep upping the ante.

58:00.320 --> 58:13.260
To get the exact same baseline level of surprise out of an audience today that a director got 30 years ago, modern creators have to constantly increase the intensity, the violence, the pacing, or resort to meta-ironic self-awareness.

58:13.580 --> 58:18.060
The baseline required to penetrate the audience's modal fatigue keeps rising.

58:18.060 --> 58:22.380
And how does this connect back to modality heading in our intellectual lives?

58:22.740 --> 58:27.140
Because watching an action movie is one thing, but how does surprise heading trap our thinking?

58:27.620 --> 58:31.200
Because we can develop surprise heading in our intellectual and political diets.

58:31.460 --> 58:39.920
We start relying on the feeling of a sudden reversal or a counterintuitive hot take as a substitute for actual explanatory power.

58:40.020 --> 58:42.040
Oh, I see this all the time on social media.

58:42.220 --> 58:43.380
The contrarian take.

58:43.380 --> 58:43.920
Exactly.

58:44.340 --> 58:48.040
We start valuing ideas simply because they are transgressive or shocking.

58:48.840 --> 58:55.320
If an idea is predictable, stable, and traditionally true, we dig it intellectually inferior or boring.

58:55.520 --> 58:56.360
Just because it's old.

58:56.500 --> 58:59.720
We mistake the jolt of unexpectedness for the depth of truth.

59:00.300 --> 59:07.000
A culture can become enclosed by compulsory novelty where every stable form has to be smashed before we will pay attention to it.

59:07.360 --> 59:10.100
The shock becomes the only epistemic warrant we accept.

59:10.100 --> 59:13.200
Okay, so if we take a step back, the traps are literally everywhere.

59:13.380 --> 59:14.420
They are ubiquitous.

59:14.920 --> 59:17.540
The fonts on a page trick our brains with fake authority.

59:17.840 --> 59:18.160
Yeah.

59:18.820 --> 59:22.560
Comforting sitcom formats protect us but isolate us from reality.

59:23.400 --> 59:27.060
AI smooths the world into a personalized bubble that makes us hate messy humans.

59:27.120 --> 59:27.280
Yep.

59:27.580 --> 59:32.480
And our media diets demand constant escalating shock value just to keep us awake.

59:33.200 --> 59:34.020
It feels pretty bleak.

59:34.080 --> 59:34.880
It does sound dire.

59:35.120 --> 59:37.160
But the paper doesn't just leave us in despair.

59:37.720 --> 59:39.040
It moves into a diagnosis.

59:39.040 --> 59:39.600
Yeah.

59:39.860 --> 59:44.240
How do you, the person listening right now, know if you are enclosed?

59:44.700 --> 59:45.020
Yes.

59:45.280 --> 59:48.900
Fliction provides six specific diagnostic tests.

59:49.060 --> 59:53.320
They are practical criteria to help you evaluate the health of your own media ecology.

59:53.520 --> 59:54.120
Let's run through them.

59:54.220 --> 59:57.100
The first is the translation residue test.

59:57.180 --> 59:57.720
Break that down.

59:57.720 --> 01:00:02.180
Every single time you translate information from one format to another, from a book to

01:00:02.180 --> 01:00:07.040
a movie, from data to a chart, something is inevitably lost or distorted.

01:00:07.960 --> 01:00:08.500
The residue.

01:00:08.660 --> 01:00:10.160
The stuff left on the cutting room floor.

01:00:10.160 --> 01:00:11.880
The test is simply this.

01:00:12.420 --> 01:00:14.420
Can you identify what was left behind?

01:00:14.860 --> 01:00:19.400
If you watch a brilliantly animated five-minute explainer video on YouTube about the causes

01:00:19.400 --> 01:00:24.680
of World War I, do you realize that you just lost all the unresolved historical ambiguity,

01:00:25.260 --> 01:00:28.880
the conflicting primary sources, and the sheer scale of the tragedy?

01:00:29.280 --> 01:00:30.000
Probably not.

01:00:30.040 --> 01:00:34.060
If the fluency and the music of the video make you forget that the residue even exists,

01:00:34.160 --> 01:00:37.100
if you think you now know the whole truth, you are approaching enclosure.

01:00:37.380 --> 01:00:38.840
The second test is the exit test.

01:00:38.840 --> 01:00:40.120
This one is brutal.

01:00:40.480 --> 01:00:41.140
Very revealing.

01:00:41.360 --> 01:00:46.220
If you remove the preferred medium, do you still have capabilities or do you collapse?

01:00:46.700 --> 01:00:52.280
If your AI chatbot server goes down, can you still read the source material and synthesize

01:00:52.280 --> 01:00:52.760
it yourself?

01:00:53.280 --> 01:00:55.260
That's a scary question for a lot of people.

01:00:55.400 --> 01:01:00.600
If you take away the visual diagram, can you still explain the math concept using logic?

01:01:01.160 --> 01:01:05.760
It's testing whether the medium is a scaffold that is building your skills like training

01:01:05.760 --> 01:01:09.860
wheels, or if it has become a wheelchair that you can no longer walk without.

01:01:10.540 --> 01:01:12.080
The third is the resistance test.

01:01:12.680 --> 01:01:17.600
When you encounter ambiguity, contradiction, or unwelcome evidence in your life, can you

01:01:17.600 --> 01:01:18.240
sit with it?

01:01:18.340 --> 01:01:19.620
Can you tolerate the friction?

01:01:20.100 --> 01:01:21.580
Most of us just pull out our phones.

01:01:21.920 --> 01:01:24.640
Or do you immediately run to a format that will resolve the discomfort?

01:01:25.180 --> 01:01:28.500
Do you demand a quick podcast summary that makes the contradiction go away?

01:01:28.500 --> 01:01:32.100
A permeable modality allows the friction of reality to persist.

01:01:32.760 --> 01:01:36.300
An enclosure immediately reformats reality until it is agreeable.

01:01:36.360 --> 01:01:38.260
Number four is the cross-modal test.

01:01:38.860 --> 01:01:41.480
Do you test claims by looking at them in different formats?

01:01:41.660 --> 01:01:42.620
This is huge.

01:01:42.960 --> 01:01:48.880
If you read a really persuasive prose argument, do you ever try to map it out logically on a

01:01:48.880 --> 01:01:50.400
whiteboard to see if it holds up?

01:01:50.800 --> 01:01:56.360
If an infographic looks incredibly clean, do you ever go read the messy raw data behind

01:01:56.360 --> 01:01:58.660
it?

01:01:58.660 --> 01:02:01.840
Because if we accept that no single format gives you the whole truth, you have to cross-reference

01:02:01.840 --> 01:02:02.120
them.

01:02:02.440 --> 01:02:04.140
The fifth is the alterity test.

01:02:04.300 --> 01:02:07.660
This is arguably the most crucial for our current era of AI.

01:02:08.000 --> 01:02:08.340
Definitely.

01:02:08.660 --> 01:02:13.640
Can you tolerate constraints and human independence that are not tailored to you?

01:02:13.740 --> 01:02:18.960
Can you handle an interlocutor, a friend, a partner, a coworker who refuses your framing,

01:02:19.500 --> 01:02:21.820
changes the subject, or just gets tired?

01:02:22.000 --> 01:02:22.760
People are messy.

01:02:22.760 --> 01:02:27.500
If human friction feels to you like a user interface error that needs to be fixed, you

01:02:27.500 --> 01:02:29.160
are failing the alterity test.

01:02:29.460 --> 01:02:32.640
And the final one, number six, the functional concentration test.

01:02:33.400 --> 01:02:34.980
This is the one that really hit me where I live.

01:02:35.080 --> 01:02:35.780
It's a tough one.

01:02:35.960 --> 01:02:40.600
How many vital life functions, information gathering, emotional regulation, financial

01:02:40.600 --> 01:02:45.960
planning, memory storage, entertainment, socializing, are all tied up in one single interface?

01:02:46.120 --> 01:02:46.960
Basically everything.

01:02:46.960 --> 01:02:53.020
If my smartphone is my map, my bank, my social life, my camera, my news source, and my therapist,

01:02:53.660 --> 01:02:59.420
the cost of exiting that single modality is essentially the destruction of my entire

01:02:59.420 --> 01:03:00.080
infrastructure.

01:03:00.720 --> 01:03:02.960
It's like living in a digital company account.

01:03:03.500 --> 01:03:07.060
If the company closes the general store, you starve.

01:03:07.060 --> 01:03:10.620
That is a perfect analogy for personal enclosure.

01:03:11.420 --> 01:03:15.920
But Fliction points out that this functional concentration doesn't just happen to individuals.

01:03:16.360 --> 01:03:18.760
It happens at scale to institutions.

01:03:19.240 --> 01:03:19.640
How so?

01:03:19.840 --> 01:03:24.340
Think about massive bureaucracies, legal systems, or corporate HR departments.

01:03:24.840 --> 01:03:30.680
They build standardized forms, digital dashboards, and quantitative metrics to coordinate action

01:03:30.680 --> 01:03:32.140
across thousands of employees.

01:03:32.300 --> 01:03:33.320
Which is an affordance.

01:03:33.460 --> 01:03:34.360
That is an affordance.

01:03:34.460 --> 01:03:35.660
It makes management possible.

01:03:35.660 --> 01:03:37.340
Then the map replaces the territory.

01:03:37.560 --> 01:03:37.940
Exactly.

01:03:38.360 --> 01:03:42.020
Over time, the institution begins to believe that the dashboard is reality.

01:03:42.460 --> 01:03:47.380
If a complex human problem, say, employee burnout, or a nuanced legal dispute cannot

01:03:47.380 --> 01:03:52.140
be neatly entered into the drop-down menu of the administrative form, it literally becomes

01:03:52.140 --> 01:03:53.420
invisible to the institution.

01:03:53.500 --> 01:03:54.460
And it just doesn't compute.

01:03:54.820 --> 01:03:58.360
The institution cannot act on it because it cannot perceive it.

01:03:58.820 --> 01:04:00.920
The representation replaces the world.

01:04:01.340 --> 01:04:03.260
That is institutional enclosure.

01:04:03.260 --> 01:04:06.760
That explains so much about modern healthcare and corporate life.

01:04:06.960 --> 01:04:10.620
If you don't fit the cells on the spreadsheet, you just don't exist.

01:04:10.720 --> 01:04:11.620
You fall through the cracks.

01:04:12.100 --> 01:04:12.320
Okay.

01:04:12.380 --> 01:04:14.140
So we are heavily dependent on these forms.

01:04:14.360 --> 01:04:16.700
We are embedded in these digital company towns.

01:04:17.040 --> 01:04:20.700
And we aren't going to just throw our phones in the river, abandon society, and go live in

01:04:20.700 --> 01:04:21.020
the woods.

01:04:21.220 --> 01:04:22.360
No, that's not the point.

01:04:22.480 --> 01:04:27.440
The paper is very clear that it is not advocating for some primitive ludism.

01:04:27.440 --> 01:04:29.760
So what is the actual cure?

01:04:30.000 --> 01:04:31.060
How do we survive this?

01:04:31.520 --> 01:04:34.920
This brings us to the final concept, cross-modal literacy.

01:04:35.220 --> 01:04:35.400
Yes.

01:04:36.180 --> 01:04:42.360
The goal of this theory is not to achieve some pure, unmediated, direct contact with reality

01:04:42.360 --> 01:04:45.800
that is biologically and epistemically impossible.

01:04:45.980 --> 01:04:46.580
We're not gods.

01:04:47.000 --> 01:04:48.940
Human perception itself is a modality.

01:04:49.760 --> 01:04:51.020
Language is a modality.

01:04:51.440 --> 01:04:52.860
We will always use filters.

01:04:53.480 --> 01:04:55.920
The cure is cross-modal literacy.

01:04:55.920 --> 01:05:01.780
The cultivated capacity to inhabit a form, to know exactly what mathematical or narrative

01:05:01.780 --> 01:05:07.120
operation it is performing, to recognize what it is actively excluding, and to compare

01:05:07.120 --> 01:05:10.300
it with other forms without treating its absence as the end of the world.

01:05:10.660 --> 01:05:13.420
The paper lists a few requirements for this literacy.

01:05:13.680 --> 01:05:15.840
First is declared preservation criteria.

01:05:16.060 --> 01:05:16.300
Right.

01:05:16.440 --> 01:05:20.380
Basically, just being intellectually honest about what a translation is trying to say.

01:05:20.380 --> 01:05:25.920
If I summarize a 500-page novel for you in five minutes, I am preserving the basic plot,

01:05:26.240 --> 01:05:29.000
but I am utterly destroying the prose style and the pacing.

01:05:29.400 --> 01:05:33.560
We just need to declare what is being preserved and what is being torched in the fire.

01:05:33.660 --> 01:05:34.740
Honesty about the residue.

01:05:35.120 --> 01:05:38.260
Second is maintaining that productive friction we talked about.

01:05:38.620 --> 01:05:41.760
Refusing to let AI smooth over the hard parts of reality.

01:05:41.760 --> 01:05:43.740
And third is alternation.

01:05:44.300 --> 01:05:49.240
Using multiple partial representations side by side, rather than hunting for one magical

01:05:49.240 --> 01:05:52.260
universal translator that explains everything perfectly.

01:05:52.380 --> 01:05:53.040
That is the key.

01:05:53.340 --> 01:05:59.560
You know, I'm trying to picture this cross-modal literacy in my head, and it's almost biological.

01:05:59.800 --> 01:06:00.960
It's like how our eyes work.

01:06:01.540 --> 01:06:02.380
Binocular vision.

01:06:02.520 --> 01:06:03.220
Oh, I like that.

01:06:03.220 --> 01:06:06.980
If I close my left eye, I see a flat, two-dimensional image of the world.

01:06:07.240 --> 01:06:10.180
If I close my right eye, I see a slightly different flat image.

01:06:10.500 --> 01:06:12.000
I need both eyes open.

01:06:12.520 --> 01:06:17.660
And specifically, I need the divergence, the slight disagreement between the left eye and

01:06:17.660 --> 01:06:21.560
the right eye for my brain to actually calculate depth perception.

01:06:21.700 --> 01:06:23.380
The difference creates the depth.

01:06:23.400 --> 01:06:25.860
Is that what we are supposed to do with media formats?

01:06:26.760 --> 01:06:32.180
Let's say the left eye is a cold, hard mathematical equation, and the right eye is a highly emotional

01:06:32.180 --> 01:06:33.140
narrative story.

01:06:34.220 --> 01:06:37.240
Neither eye sees the complete objective truth on its own.

01:06:37.780 --> 01:06:39.440
They're both partial, distorted views.

01:06:40.040 --> 01:06:42.280
But the friction between them creates depth.

01:06:42.720 --> 01:06:47.000
That is an incredibly powerful analogy, and it perfectly encapsulates the thesis, because

01:06:47.000 --> 01:06:49.780
cross-modal literacy is absolutely not relativism.

01:06:49.920 --> 01:06:51.080
It's not just saying everything is fake.

01:06:51.440 --> 01:06:51.800
No.

01:06:52.160 --> 01:06:56.760
It's not post-modern nihilism saying, well, every representation is equally distorted,

01:06:56.900 --> 01:06:57.520
so who cares?

01:06:57.660 --> 01:06:58.260
Nothing is true.

01:06:58.380 --> 01:06:58.600
No.

01:06:59.140 --> 01:07:03.200
A map that places Chicago in the middle of the Pacific Ocean is a demonstration of

01:07:03.200 --> 01:07:04.320
a relatively bad map.

01:07:04.460 --> 01:07:05.560
Truth still exists.

01:07:05.560 --> 01:07:08.900
But modal plurality means asking rigorous questions.

01:07:09.380 --> 01:07:11.560
What is this specific map designed to show?

01:07:12.220 --> 01:07:12.660
Elevation?

01:07:13.120 --> 01:07:13.620
Highways?

01:07:14.120 --> 01:07:14.880
Political boundaries?

01:07:15.560 --> 01:07:16.480
What is the operation?

01:07:16.760 --> 01:07:17.820
What are its invariants?

01:07:17.920 --> 01:07:19.660
And what forms of correction does it permit?

01:07:19.660 --> 01:07:24.600
The ultimate design principle for our technology, and for our own minds, should be this.

01:07:25.220 --> 01:07:28.780
We must adapt the presentation to the user without adapting away the object.

01:07:29.080 --> 01:07:32.080
The resistance of the real world must survive the interface.

01:07:32.080 --> 01:07:34.480
We have covered a staggering amount of ground today.

01:07:34.580 --> 01:07:35.440
A huge amount.

01:07:35.580 --> 01:07:40.360
We started with Claude Shannon's 1948 information theory, and destroyed the myth of the neutral

01:07:40.360 --> 01:07:40.700
pipe.

01:07:41.080 --> 01:07:45.940
We looked at how the literal shapes of fonts can hijack our dopamine systems and our sense

01:07:45.940 --> 01:07:47.860
of profundity through typeface heading.

01:07:48.020 --> 01:07:49.660
The distressed denim of the mind.

01:07:49.820 --> 01:07:55.460
We explored how formats act as protective blankets, like a 90s sitcom masking trauma, and how they act

01:07:55.460 --> 01:07:59.620
as constitutive scaffolds, like a theatrical superhero cape organizing bravery.

01:07:59.620 --> 01:08:02.140
From Mr. Robot to Darkwing Duck.

01:08:02.680 --> 01:08:09.360
We stare down the terrifying, asymmetric comfort of AI chatbots that perfectly adapt to our needs,

01:08:10.020 --> 01:08:14.720
while slowly making us intolerant of messy, independent human beings.

01:08:15.580 --> 01:08:21.020
And we ended with the necessity of cross-modal literacy, the ability to hold multiple conflicting

01:08:21.020 --> 01:08:24.000
formats in our minds without being trapped by any of them.

01:08:24.280 --> 01:08:27.640
It is a profound shift in how we must evaluate our environment.

01:08:27.640 --> 01:08:31.780
The next time you find yourself gravitating toward a specific type of explainer video,

01:08:32.040 --> 01:08:37.640
or a perfectly curated conversational summary, or an AI chat that just completely gets you.

01:08:37.740 --> 01:08:38.200
The boss.

01:08:38.380 --> 01:08:39.080
Just take a second.

01:08:39.480 --> 01:08:41.700
Ask yourself, what is the operation happening here?

01:08:41.820 --> 01:08:43.240
What residue is being left behind?

01:08:43.360 --> 01:08:46.680
And most importantly, am I losing my tolerance for the friction of reality?

01:08:46.920 --> 01:08:50.540
I want to leave you with a final provocative thought to mull over, building on the trajectory

01:08:50.540 --> 01:08:51.220
of this paper.

01:08:51.820 --> 01:08:56.100
Right now, we consume all these modalities through screens and headphones and paper.

01:08:56.160 --> 01:08:57.080
External tools.

01:08:57.080 --> 01:08:58.720
They're external to our bodies.

01:08:59.480 --> 01:09:04.940
But as brain-computer interfaces, neural implants, and advanced augmented reality become mainstream

01:09:04.940 --> 01:09:09.200
in the coming years, the modality won't just be a rectangle you hold in your hand.

01:09:09.240 --> 01:09:09.940
No, it won't.

01:09:10.000 --> 01:09:13.420
The interface will be overlaid directly onto your visual cortex.

01:09:14.120 --> 01:09:18.320
It will interpret your brain waves before you even form a conscious thought.

01:09:18.320 --> 01:09:25.000
If we do not develop this cross-modal literacy now, the personalized modality and closure of

01:09:25.000 --> 01:09:27.820
the future won't just dictate what articles you read.

01:09:28.080 --> 01:09:32.580
It will literally rewrite the physics of the reality you perceive in real time.

01:09:32.580 --> 01:09:34.080
This is a terrifying prospect.

